@@ -52,3 +52,19 @@ and a non-object target is treated as `{}` when an object patch is applied. As t
 merge patch cannot set a member to `null`. It never throws. Neither argument is mutated, and the result
 shares no structure with them. Only own members are read and `__proto__` is an ordinary member, so
 prototypes are never reached.
+
+## Creating a patch
+
+`createPatch(from, to)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, returns a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that turns `from`
+into `to` when applied with `applyPatch`.
+
+```ts
+createPatch({ a: 1, b: [1, 2, 3] }, { a: 1, b: [1, 2, 9, 3] }); // [{ op: "add", path: "/b/2", value: 9 }]
+```
+
+The patch holds only what changed, using `add`, `remove` and `replace`: unchanged members and items are
+left out and a changed member is patched in place. Arrays are compared by longest common subsequence, so
+an item gained or lost is a single `add` or `remove`, and a moved item is a `remove` plus an `add`, without
+touching the items around it. Equal documents (member order ignored) give `[]`; a root of a different type
+gives a `replace` at path `""`. Neither argument is mutated, the patch shares no structure with them, and
+keys are escaped per RFC 6901, with `__proto__` an ordinary member.
